WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.080
Tap the tendon below your kneecap, and your leg kicks before you decide anything.

2
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.020
How long does that take, and where does the time go?

3
00:00:13.083 --> 00:00:15.563
The signal runs a route with five stations.

4
00:00:16.963 --> 00:00:19.983
First, a receptor in the thigh muscle detects the stretch.

5
00:00:21.183 --> 00:00:24.763
Then a sensory neuron carries the signal up to the spinal cord.

6
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:30.077
In the spinal cord, the integrating centre, it crosses one synapse.

7
00:00:31.237 --> 00:00:33.697
A motor neuron carries it back down the leg.

8
00:00:34.897 --> 00:00:37.337
Then the effector, the thigh muscle, contracts.

9
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.290
Zoom in on the motor neuron.

10
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:45.130
Its dendrites receive signals, and the cell body gathers them.

11
00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:51.650
One long axon carries the signal away, in a large mammal sometimes a metre or more.

12
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.310
Its terminals pass it on.

13
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.970
Along the axon the signal is an impulse.

14
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.130
It is all or none: full size, or nothing at all.

15
00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.910
On many axons it hops between short bare gaps in a wrapping called myelin.

16
00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.930
At most synapses, the terminal releases a chemical that crosses a narrow gap.

17
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.050
But how fast does the impulse run?

18
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.730
In 1850, Hermann von Helmholtz asked that of a frog.

19
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.450
He stimulated its nerve near the calf muscle and timed the twitch.

20
00:01:27.583 --> 00:01:31.683
Then he moved the stimulus farther up the same nerve, and timed it again.

21
00:01:33.003 --> 00:01:35.863
Every time, the twitch came a little later than before.

22
00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:40.447
Each delay includes time at the junction and in the muscle.

23
00:01:41.747 --> 00:01:44.867
One part of these two delays belongs to the nerve alone.

24
00:01:46.307 --> 00:01:47.307
Which part?

25
00:01:53.317 --> 00:01:59.417
The difference. The junction and the muscle take the same time in both trials, so

26
00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:04.457
they cancel. The extra delay belongs to the extra length of nerve.

27
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:10.427
Divide that extra length by that extra time, and you get the nerve's own speed.

28
00:02:11.887 --> 00:02:16.027
Helmholtz found a few tens of metres per second, about a car on a motorway.

29
00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:18.317
Now back to the knee.

30
00:02:19.657 --> 00:02:22.757
Each axon's time is its length divided by its speed.

31
00:02:24.057 --> 00:02:27.797
In this model, each synapse in the cord adds about a millisecond.

32
00:02:28.817 --> 00:02:31.217
The junction and the muscle add a little more.

33
00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:37.187
The bar's legs add up to just under the measured time, when the thigh muscle's

34
00:02:37.187 --> 00:02:38.547
electrical signal starts.

35
00:02:39.827 --> 00:02:43.327
The bar leaves out the brief tap and stretch before the spindle fires.

36
00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:46.167
The kick itself comes later.

37
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.027
Nearly all of the bar is time on the two axons.

38
00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.930
So a response is a route, and its time is the sum of its legs.

39
00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:00.010
Change a length, a speed, or the number of synapses, and the time changes.
